.TH ECHOD 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
echod \- TCP echo service daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B echod
.RI [ port ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B echod
is a minimal implementation of the Echo Protocol.
It listens for TCP connections on
.I port
(default 7) on all IPv4 addresses and sends back every byte it receives
on a connection until the client closes it.
.PP
Each accepted connection is served by its own thread, created when the
connection is accepted and gone when the client closes it, so any number of
clients can be connected at once; there is no fixed pool of workers.
If a thread cannot be created, that one connection is closed and the daemon
goes on accepting others.
The listen queue is
.BR SOMAXCONN .
There is no UDP service.
.PP
.B echod
does not detach from its terminal; it runs in the foreground and prints
a line to standard output when it starts listening and when each connection
is accepted and closed.
It is not started by any of the boot scripts.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I port
TCP port to listen on.
The argument is converted with
.BR atoi (3)
and is not otherwise checked.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.BR accept (2)
failed with an error other than
.BR EINTR .
.TP
.B 1
The socket could not be created, bound or put into the listening state.
.SH EXAMPLE
Serve the echo protocol on port 7007 and test it:
.EX
# echod 7007 &
$ telnet localhost 7007
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR telnetd (8),
.BR tcp (7)
.SH STANDARDS
RFC 862 (Echo Protocol), TCP service only.
.SH BUGS
.B echod
does not ignore
.BR SIGPIPE ,
so a client that closes its connection while data is being echoed back can
terminate the daemon, and with it every other client's connection.
